{
  "dom_id": "step-q129950",
  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "Calculate ${∬}_{R}(2-2x+9{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A$,  where $R=[0,2]\\times [-1,2].$",
  "worked_solution": "Using the linearity property of double integrals, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}(2-2x+9{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{R}2\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{R}2x\\text{ }\\text{d}A+{∬}_{R}9{y}^{2}\\text{ }\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first integral is the double integral of a constant, so we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}2\\,\\text{d}A & =2{∬}_{R}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2\\text{Area}(R) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (2-(-1)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe function under the second integral is independent of $y.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}2x\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =(2-(-1)){\\int }_{0}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3[{x}^{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3({2}^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the third integral is independent of $x.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}9{y}^{2}\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =(2-0){\\int }_{-1}^{2}9{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2[3{y}^{3}]_{-1}^{2} \\\\  & =6({2}^{3}-(-1)^{3}) \\\\  & =54.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, combining our results, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}(2-2x+9{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A=12-12+54=54.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
